  "excerpt": "How edge computing fits modern web architecture: latency reduction, CDN functions, and serverless edge runtimes. Short overview of benefits and adoption patterns.",
  "content": "<p>Edge computing represents a fundamental shift in how we distribute computational resources across the globe. Rather than relying solely on centralized data centers, edge computing brings computation closer to the end user, reducing latency and improving overall application responsiveness.</p><p>The benefits are compelling: latency is typically reduced from hundreds of milliseconds to single-digit milliseconds, opening up possibilities for real-time applications that were previously impractical. Imagine video conferencing with virtually no perceptible delay, or real-time collaborative editing tools that respond instantaneously to user input.</p><p>Cloudflare Workers, AWS Lambda@Edge, and Netlify Edge Functions have made edge computing accessible to developers without requiring deep infrastructure expertise. These platforms abstract away the complexity of deploying and managing edge locations globally, allowing developers to focus on writing application logic.</p><p>Use cases have expanded dramatically: authentication and authorization at the edge, content personalization based on geographic location or device type, real-time image optimization, and adaptive streaming for media delivery. Fraud detection systems can validate transactions milliseconds after they occur, while maintaining complete user data locality for privacy compliance.</p><p>The future of web architecture will increasingly leverage edge computing as a core component. Organizations that master this pattern will gain significant competitive advantages in terms of user experience and operational efficiency.</p>"
